# next-app/.env.example — every environment variable the site reads, with what it does and
# where it is set. Copy to .env.local for development. Nothing here is a value.
#
# Vercel: Project → Settings → Environment Variables. Anything marked "server only" must NOT
# be exposed with a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, and the Supabase secret key should be scoped to
# Production only (FIN-E05: preview deployments are production Supabase clients).

# ── Supabase (server only) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used by app/api/lead/route.ts (Phase 1d) and app/api/chat/route.ts (the support record and
# signed-in verification). The site never talks to Supabase from the browser.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ── Lead capture (growth plan W2 Tranche B; off until the owner applies the migration) ─────
# Needs supabase/migrations/20260915120000_leads_and_placement_locality.sql applied first, then
# both set together (the form and the API must agree) — the owner's call. With the CHATWOOT_*
# values below also set, every lead opens a conversation in the team's inbox.
# LEADS_ENABLED=true
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LEADS_ENABLED=true

# ── Support assistant (server only) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Without these the assistant still answers — from the retrieval layer, unphrased (rung 3).
# With them a model phrases the answer, subject to the number gate in lib/assistant/answer.ts.
#
# The chain is configuration, never code: free models rotate out, and the privacy policy names
# the providers, so changing it is a release step with a policy amendment. The OpenRouter
# account must have "allow providers that may train on inputs" OFF for free models before the
# key is used here; that setting is what makes Privacy Policy 2.0 §3.6 true.
ASSISTANT_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
ASSISTANT_API_KEY=
ASSISTANT_MODELS=google/gemma-4-31b-it:free,google/gemma-4-26b-a4b-it:free,dots-studio/dots-3-note-preview:free
# Soft daily cap on model calls per server instance; past it, rung 3 serves. Default 800.
# OpenRouter's own cap is the real one: 50 calls/day on an account with no purchase history
# (the owner's choice for now, 2026-09-11), 1,000/day permanently after a one-time $10 credit
# purchase. Past either cap the provider returns 429 and the assistant serves the retrieved
# answer unphrased — still correct, just not conversational. The record shows how often.
# ASSISTANT_DAILY_MODEL_CALLS=800
# Extra JSON merged into every model request, for a provider that needs something non-standard.
# On openrouter.ai the default is {"reasoning":{"enabled":false}} — some free models are reasoning
# models and otherwise spend the whole token budget thinking and return no text at all.
# ASSISTANT_EXTRA_BODY={"reasoning":{"enabled":false}}
# Origins allowed to call /api/chat cross-origin. The site and the booking app are same-origin;
# the native app (Initiative 1) will need its origin added here.
# ASSISTANT_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://mybuddymaid.in,https://www.mybuddymaid.in

# ── Maintenance (deliberately unset) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Setting this rewrites every route to /maintenance.
# MAINTENANCE_MODE=1

# ── Chatwoot Cloud (server only) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The support inbox the team answers from (owner decision 3). On escalation the assistant
# creates a conversation there with the transcript, forwards what the customer types next,
# and the widget polls Chatwoot for the team's replies. The inbox's own webhook (Inboxes →
# Website assistant → Settings: Webhook URL = https://<site>/api/chatwoot/webhook) writes
# agent replies back into the support record. WhatsApp/email reach the record only through an
# account-level webhook (Settings → Integrations → Webhooks), where the plan has one.
# Account id, inbox id and the inbox identifier (Inboxes → Website assistant → Configuration)
# are visible in the app and are not secrets. The inbox identifier keys Chatwoot's token-free
# Client API, which creates the contact and conversation and carries the customer's side.
# CHATWOOT_WEBHOOK_SECRET is the Webhook Secret beside that Webhook URL. Each sender signs
# with its own secret, so only one sender may point at the route.
# CHATWOOT_API_TOKEN is an agent bot's token. Leave it unset on the free tier, which refuses
# the bot every Application API call ("API access is not enabled for this account"); the
# assistant's turns are then posted from the visitor's side with an "Assistant: " prefix.
# Leave the inbox's Bot Configuration empty too: a bot on the inbox parks every new
# conversation as pending until the bot opens it, which that token cannot do.
# CHATWOOT_HMAC_TOKEN is only needed if the inbox enforces user identity validation.
CHATWOOT_API_URL=https://app.chatwoot.com
CHATWOOT_ACCOUNT_ID=
CHATWOOT_INBOX_ID=
CHATWOOT_INBOX_IDENTIFIER=
CHATWOOT_API_TOKEN=
CHATWOOT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# CHATWOOT_HMAC_TOKEN=

# ── Search Console, Bing and analytics tooling (scripts only; never read by the site) ──────
# The growth pipeline (scripts/growth/*, docs/growth/README.md) and the SEO scripts. Locally
# these are set in the PowerShell session for one run; in GitHub Actions they are repository
# secrets of the same names (SUPABASE_URL stands in for NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL there).
# GSC_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=   # path to the Google service-account key file, or the key JSON itself
# GSC_SITE=                   # default sc-domain:mybuddymaid.in
# GA4_PROPERTY_ID=            # numeric; `npm run growth:ga4 -- --list-properties` prints it
# BING_WEBMASTER_API_KEY=     # Bing Webmaster Tools -> Settings -> API access
# BING_SITE_URL=              # optional; otherwise resolved from GetUserSites
# GROWTH_END=                 # window end YYYY-MM-DD; default three days before today
# GROWTH_DIR=                 # output root; default docs/growth
# GROWTH_RAW_DIR=             # when set, raw API responses are dumped there (CI artifacts)
# INDEXNOW_KEY=               # scripts/seo/indexnow.ts; the key file is committed under public/
# BASE_URL=                   # scripts/seo/crawl.ts and check-redirects.ts target
# VERCEL_AUTOMATION_BYPASS_SECRET=  # only for crawling a protected preview deployment
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=          # scripts/seo/draft-copy.ts (manual drafting only, never at build)

# ── Site verification meta tags (read by app/layout.tsx) ──────────────────────────────────
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BING_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# ── Open Graph image signing (server only) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Any long random string. When set, /og renders only the titles the site itself signed
# (lib/seo-engine/og-sign.ts); unset, the endpoint accepts anything, which keeps local
# development working. Set it in Vercel for Production and Preview.
# OG_SIGNING_SECRET=
